 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d047: knot (k=7)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 2.0 and radius 1.64 with 7 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.36) and a 4-dim charge; 5 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 24 couplings (58% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 0.96 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 0.59 bonds per particle, largest molecule 3.0 particles, 14.5 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.103 vs ESN 0.999\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.096  random bonds 0.104\n  xor      0.590 vs ESN 0.514\u00b10.003  [beat]  no bonds 0.451  random bonds 0.540\n  narma10  0.315 vs ESN 0.844\u00b10.001  [below]  no bonds 0.278  random bonds 0.320\n  latch    0.842 vs ESN 0.678\u00b10.003  [beat]  no bonds 0.844  random bonds 0.872\n  mnist14  0.527 vs ESN 0.830\u00b10.007  [below]  no bonds 0.504  random bonds 0.517\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly matters on xor: the bonds its copies chose beat both no bonds and random bonds.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
